# Which CurseForge project a tag uploads to. The BigWigs packager reads this
# line and nothing else to find the project - without it a tagged build runs,
# succeeds, and uploads nowhere. Public information; the API key that goes
# with it is a repository secret and is never in this file.
## X-Curse-Project-ID: 1644286

# THE SAME FOR WAGO. An 8-character alphanumeric id from the developer
# dashboard at addons.wago.io/developers - NOT the slug in the addon's URL,
# which is a different string that happens to look like a name.
#
# THIS LINE IS HALF OF IT. The other half is the WAGO_API_TOKEN secret in the
# repository's Actions settings; a token cannot carry the project id and this
# file cannot carry the token. With only one of the two the packager skips
# Wago and says so - it does not fail the build, and CurseForge is unaffected.
## X-Wago-ID: 96ELogNg

# Embedded libraries. Not a design decision reversed - LibSharedMedia is a
# REGISTRY, not a look: it is how the fonts and textures the user already has
# from their other addons show up in our pickers, under the names they know.
# Licences stay with the libraries: LibStub is public domain, CallbackHandler
# is BSD, LibSharedMedia is LGPL 2.1, LibSerialize is MIT and LibDeflate is
# zlib. Load order is dependency order.
#
# LibSerialize and LibDeflate are the pair every addon that shares a string
# uses - WeakAuras, Plater, MDT and EllesmereUI all reach for exactly these
# two. Serialize turns a table into bytes, Deflate squeezes them and turns
# them into something you can paste into a chat window. Writing either by hand
# would be a worse copy of a solved problem.
#
# NOTE ON THE LIBDEFLATE LICENCE: the copy shipped beside LibDeflate in one of
# the addons on this machine was the LGPL, which is NOT its licence -
# LibDeflate.lua says zlib in its own header, and the LICENSE.txt here is the
# matching one. Copying the file that happens to sit next to a library is how
# a project ends up claiming the wrong terms.
Libs\LibStub\LibStub.lua
Libs\CallbackHandler-1.0\CallbackHandler-1.0.lua
Libs\LibSharedMedia-3.0\LibSharedMedia-3.0.lua
Libs\LibSerialize\LibSerialize.lua
Libs\LibDeflate\LibDeflate.lua

# THE LANGUAGE, ABOVE EVERYTHING. Not a module and not a feature: ns.L has to
# answer from the first line of the addon onwards, because the first thing
# that could ask it is the second file. Languages.lua is only data and loads
# straight after the engine that reads it.
#
# NOTHING LOOKS A STRING UP AT FILE SCOPE. At file scope the profile is not
# open, so the answer would be English and then frozen for the session - the
# self test checks the tables, and the rule is written at the top of
# Core/Locale.lua.
Core\Locale.lua
Core\Languages.lua

Core\Init.lua

# Turning a layout into a string and back. High up because Bars.lua uses its
# adopt rule - the one that re-issues ids and remaps anchors - and that rule
# has to exist before anything that copies a bar.
Core\Share.lua

# Which settings a character is using. Owns ns.OpenProfile, which every file
# after this one reads the result of through ns.db.
Core\Profiles.lua
# Which features are running at all. Above everything it switches and below
# the profile it reads, because a module switch is a per-character setting.
# It holds no frame and touches nothing at load - the entries reach for
# ns.Screen and friends only when a switch is actually flipped.
Core\Modules.lua

# Which channel a message actually goes on, and how a sentence is written.
# Above everything that says anything: the externals panel, the taunt
# announce and whatever asks the group something next. One answer to "am I in
# a party or an instance group", because getting that wrong sends a message
# nowhere at all and says nothing about it.
Core\Chat.lua
# The addon channel. Above everything that asks or answers, and registered at
# LOAD rather than from a module's Boot: an unregistered prefix drops every
# message before any addon code sees it, and a channel that depends on which
# features somebody switched on works for one of you and not the other.
Core\Comm.lua
# Durability over the shared LibDurability wire - the channel MRT and
# BigWigs embed, spoken rather than embedded (the copy on this machine
# names no licence). Below Comm, whose channel rule it reuses at call time.
Core\Durability.lua

Core\Media.lua
# The bar textures this addon ships, registered into the SHARED registry -
# so they sit next to everything the user's other addons put there, and are
# available to those addons in return.
Core\MediaLibrary.lua
# Which noise each of four moments makes. Directly below Media, whose registry
# it reads and whose one sink it plays through, and above everything that can
# make one: the externals panel, the answer buttons, the bars' effects and the
# reminders. It holds no frame and plays nothing at load - a sound at file
# scope would fire before the profile is even open.
Core\Sounds.lua
Core\Secrets.lua
# THE COOLDOWN MANAGER, being built a second time. Everything under
# Core\Cooldowns\ belongs to it, and the folder is the point: the last
# implementation put 7 700 lines into five files at this level, and the file
# that was hardest to cut was the one nobody could describe in a sentence.
#
# Contract.lua is first and holds no feature at all - it is the five rules
# about Blizzard's frames plus the weak-keyed table that makes rule 3
# possible. Above CDM.lua, which is the reader those rules protect.
Core\Cooldowns\Contract.lua
# Who ELSE is managing cooldowns. Read at login before any module boots, and
# above CDM.lua because the answer decides whether we go near those frames at
# all. It reads addon metadata and nothing of ours, so it needs only the
# namespace above it.
Core\Cooldowns\Rivals.lua
Core\CDM.lua
# The shipped proc database. Data, and it grows with every export, so it loads
# before the logic that reads it and stays a file you can paste into.
Core\Auras.lua
# The arrangement engine, the visibility rules and the effects. All three are
# read by Bars.lua at file scope - BAR_DEFAULTS is built out of their default
# tables - so all three load above it.
Core\Layout.lua
# The cooldown lattice - where cell N of a bar sits. Pure, and BELOW
# Layout.lua because it calls Layout.Compact rather than carrying a second
# copy of the gap-closing rule.
Core\Cooldowns\Model.lua
# Reading the bars still in the profile. Below Model, whose vocabulary it
# translates into, and below Profiles - it reads ns.db and ns.SpecStore.
Core\Cooldowns\Store.lua
# Changing the bars - the write side of Store, and separate from it so
# Store's promise ("it translates, it does not rewrite") stays true on its
# own first line. Below Store, which it reads.
Core\Cooldowns\Bars.lua
# THE ONE FILE ALLOWED TO WRITE TO A FRAME BLIZZARD OWNS. Below Contract,
# whose weak-keyed record it keeps its state in, and below CDM, whose viewers
# hand it the frames. The desk guard names this path: every other file in the
# addon fails the build for a SetPoint on an adopted frame, and this one does
# not.
Core\Cooldowns\Claim.lua
# WAVE 4 AND 5 - the styling layer. Effects, Look, Text and Fill each restyle
# one adopted frame, and every one of them writes through Claim.Set rather
# than to the frame directly: Claim records what a thing looked like before
# the first change, so switching the module off puts all of it back. The desk
# guard follows a frame into its parts now, so a raw setter in any of these
# fails the build.
#
# Below Claim, which they all go through, and ABOVE Render, which drives them.
Core\Cooldowns\Effects.lua
Core\Cooldowns\Look.lua
Core\Cooldowns\Text.lua
Core\Cooldowns\Fill.lua
# WAVE 6 - the places we draw OURSELVES. A bar-shaped place is ours again and
# an icon-shaped one stays borrowed, which is 4.82.0's split: Blizzard's
# TrackedBar template lays itself out, so every setting on the bar page was
# either styling a frame that was not there or moving a plate behind one that
# would not move. Below Look, Text and Fill, all three of which it captures.
Core\Cooldowns\Own.lua
# The pass that walks a bar: Store reads it, Model places it, Claim writes it.
# LAST of the seven, because it captures all of them at file scope - this order
# is load-bearing and a desk guard fails the build if it is disturbed.
Core\Cooldowns\Render.lua
Core\Visibility.lua
# The aura engine and the co-tank frames.
#
# Engine.lua probes for AuraContainer by BUILDING one and seeing, so it is
# safe on a client that predates it - it simply reports itself unavailable
# and names the build in the reason. CoTanks then draws everything it can
# without it and says why the aura strips are empty.
Core\Engine.lua
Core\CoTanks.lua
# Reminders: text on the screen when a buff has fallen off. Below Visibility,
# whose evaluator it borrows for "only in combat", and below CDM, which is the
# only thing on this patch that can answer whether a buff is up at all.
Core\Reminders.lua
# Saying you took it, and asking the other tank to. Below CoTanks, whose page
# it puts its settings on, and below Chat, whose channel rules it shares with
# the externals panel rather than growing a second answer to "which channel
# am I in".
Core\Taunts.lua
# Own casts recorded, readiness estimated - History feeds the Timeline
# panel's strip and the death window's "was it still ready".
# Which specialisation everybody is playing. Above Externals, whose panel is
# its only reader, and above nothing else on purpose: it answers "I do not
# know" until the server says otherwise, and every caller is written to treat
# that as "show it anyway".
Core\Specs.lua
# What everybody is wearing, read off the same inspect answers Specs asks
# for - it registers with Specs at load, so it sits directly below it.
Core\Gear.lua
# The cooldowns somebody ELSE presses on you. Below Reminders because it is
# the same kind of thing - a panel with its own placement - and it needs
# nothing from anybody: class and the roster are all it reads.
Core\Externals.lua
# The other end of a request: somebody asks, a button lights up, you press it.
# Below Externals and Taunts, whose spell lists it reads to know what you can
# be asked for.
Core\Answers.lua
# The line on the screen when somebody asks: the answers' own alert, in the
# reminders' type. Below Answers (it is told by it) and Reminders (it borrows
# Extent and FlashAlpha), above EditMode, which places it.
Core\AnswerAlerts.lua
# WHAT THE MOB IN FRONT OF YOU IS CASTING, and whether it is coming at you.
# Three files, in this order: the engine that reads the nameplates, the bar
# it draws, and a third book of reminders for the alerts. Below Reminders
# (CastAlerts is an instance of that class) and Visibility (the bar carries
# the same rules every other surface does), above EditMode, which places
# both of them.
# THE SEASON'S MOBS AND BOSSES - names, NPC ids, levels and the spells they
# cast, taken from Mythic Dungeon Tools. Data only, no frame and no event, so
# it loads wherever it is needed first: above Casts.lua, which reads it.
Core\MobData.lua
Core\Casts.lua
Core\CastBar.lua
Core\CastAlerts.lua
# The group fills itself. Below Chat, whose rules about which channel you are
# actually in it shares, and it holds no frame at all - a chat event and four
# plain calls. Everything it does is off until switched on; see the file.
Core\Invites.lua
Core\History.lua
Core\Death.lua
# Everybody ELSE's deaths, off the same damage-meter list Death already reads
# and then discards. Below Death because it reads Death.ReadRecap and
# Death.StripRealm; it owns no source of its own.
Core\RaidDeaths.lua
# The death, played back on a timeline: what came in above the axis, what
# you pressed below it. Reads Death's own pure rules (Storyline, ReplayAt,
# RecentEvents) and adds no source of its own.
Core\Replay.lua
Core\Minimap.lua
# The other two ways in: the minimap button above, and an entry in the pause
# menu. Both only call ns.Options at click time, so neither needs the window
# to have loaded yet.
Core\GameMenu.lua
# Widgets defines ns.UI, and EditMode reads it while it loads. Anything that
# takes a reference to the design system at file scope belongs BELOW this line.
Core\Widgets.lua
# The raid bar and the window it opens. BELOW Widgets - both are built from
# the design system - and above Edit Mode, which drags the bar around the
# screen with the other panels.
#
# RaidBar reads ns.ICON_TEXTURE while it loads (one of its buttons opens a
# window of ours and wears our own mark), so it is below Init as well.
Core\RaidBar.lua
# The card the raid check's magnifier opens: gear, enchants and talents of
# one person. Below Widgets like every window, above RaidCheck, which puts
# the magnifier in its rows.
Core\PlayerCard.lua
Core\RaidCheck.lua
Core\EditMode.lua
# Setting a key is its own mode on the screen, next to Edit Mode and for the
# same reason: what you are deciding lives out there, not in a list. Below
# Widgets - it is built from the design system - and it reaches the two panels
# only when it opens, so their own load order does not matter here.
Core\Keys.lua
Core\Changelog.lua
# The first window a character ever sees, and the one place the modules can be
# switched without hunting for a page. Below Widgets, whose design system it
# is built from, and below Modules, whose list it draws.
Core\Welcome.lua
# What changed since you last played. Below Changelog, which is the data
# it renders, and below Welcome - a fresh install gets the welcome window
# and NOT a wall of history, and the rule that keeps them apart is in News.
Core\News.lua
# The spell chooser, lifted out of the cooldown workspace when the bars
# went: the death log and the reminders both build theirs from it, so it
# loads above them and below the two catalogues it reads.
Core\SpellPane.lua
# The "when to show it" block, built once for every page that has a rule:
# above the pages, below Widgets and Visibility, whose vocabulary it draws.
Core\OptionsWhen.lua
# The Cooldowns page. Above the rest only because it is first in the rail;
# it reads Cooldowns\Rivals.lua and CDM.lua, both of which are far above.
Core\OptionsCooldowns.lua
# The look, text, effects and fill blocks of that page, in a file of their
# own. Below it because the page CALLS them, and because the bar they edit is
# whichever one OptionsCooldowns.Current answers with - the page owns the
# selection and these blocks ask it on every read.
Core\OptionsCooldownsBar.lua
Core\OptionsCoTanks.lua
# Below OptionsBars: the reminders page borrows its spell list rather than
# growing a second one, so BuildSpellPane has to exist by the time this loads.
Core\OptionsReminders.lua
Core\OptionsExternals.lua
Core\OptionsAnswers.lua
Core\OptionsCasts.lua
Core\OptionsRaidBar.lua
Core\OptionsInvites.lua
Core\OptionsDeaths.lua
Core\OptionsProfiles.lua
Core\OptionsSettings.lua
Core\Options.lua
# Last, because it reads all of the above. /zs test - the checks that can only
# be run inside the game, which is every check this addon has.
Core\SelfTest.lua

# BINDINGS.XML IS DELIBERATELY NOT LISTED HERE. The game loads a file of that
# name from the addon's root by itself, with the parser that knows what a
# <Binding> is. Listed in the TOC it is read by the UI XML parser instead,
# which does not - and every line of it comes back as "Unrecognized XML:
# Binding" while the key never appears in the key list. Checked against MRT,
# GatherMate2, Fitter, MDTHelper and EllesmereUIActionBars: not one of them
# lists it, and the last leaves a comment saying why.

# Deleted in 4.9.0: Core\Glow.lua, the expanding proc ring. It went with the
# single-aura window in 4.4.0 and nothing has called it since - it was still
# being loaded on every login for nothing. In the git history if wanted.
#
# Deleted in 4.4.0, and not coming back: Core\Display.lua and Core\Watcher.lua,
# the single-aura window and the aura hunt behind it. An aura is a cell on a
# bar now, so a second window showing one buff on its own was two answers to
# the same question. They are in the git history if anything is ever wanted
# back out of them.

# Parked until patch 12.1. Blizzard_AuraContainer does not exist before then,
# so every one of these is inert on a 12.0 client - verified in game, the
# engine probe fails outright. They are kept, not deleted, because the code
# is correct and 12.1 is days away.
#   Core\Engine.lua        the AuraContainer layer
#   Core\Catalog.lua       spell catalogue from talents and the spellbook
#   Core\Probe.lua         "does this spell ID bind?" diagnostic
#   Core\Groups.lua        engine-backed tracking groups
#   Core\CoTanks.lua       co-tank panel with engine aura strips
#   Core\OptionsGroups.lua the options page for the above

# Removed 2026-08-17 (git history has it): Routes, the MDT pull badges on
# nameplates. Parked 2026-08-09 after measuring that a 12.0 client withholds a
# dungeon mob's name, id, health, creature type and every position; brought
# back as an experiment for 4.84.0 to ask the last doors (combat log: refused;
# a cast names the TYPE of a mob, not which one, and only once it casts). No
# door leads to "this mob is pull 7", and pull trackers without one exist.
